    "content": "George was a pioneering American political lifestyle magazine founded by John F. Kennedy Jr. and Michael J. Berman in 1995, aimed at merging politics with popular culture. With the tagline \"Not Just Politics As Usual,\" it featured celebrity-driven covers—such as Cindy Crawford as George Washington—to engage younger readers. The magazine ran until 2001, closing two years after Kennedy's death. Key Details About George Magazine: Founder and Publisher: Launched by John F. Kennedy Jr. with partner Michael J. Berman, with publishing backing from Hachette Filipacchi Media U.S.. Mission: To make politics accessible and entertaining by mixing it with celebrity, fashion, and pop culture. Key Issues/Covers: The inaugural September 1995 issue featured Cindy Crawford; other notable covers included Drew Barrymore as Marilyn Monroe, Julia Roberts, and Harrison Ford. Duration: 1995 to 2001. Closure: The magazine ceased publication in 2001 following the death of JFK Jr. and declining advertising support. While George initially had a successful launch, becoming a high-profile publication, it struggled to maintain its niche after Kennedy's passing, say the New York Times.",
